<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George VIII of Georgia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1417">1417</a>–<a href="/years/1476">1476</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as George I of Kakheti · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George VIII was the last king of a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, reigning from 1446. Regional lords challenged him: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrat-vi-of-georgia">Bagrat of Imereti</a> defeated him at Chikhori in 1463, and in 1465 the atabeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a> captured him. After his release he kept only Kakheti in the east, which he ruled as George I of Kakheti until 1476, and the kingdom split into Kartli, Kakheti, Imereti and Samtskhe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-viii-of-georgia.jpg" alt="George VIII of Georgia" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Digitized by the National Center of Manuscripts, Tbilisi, Georgia; derivative work by ... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_VIII_of_Georgia_charter_1460-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>George was the third son of Alexander I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and his second wife Tamar, daughter of Alexander I of Imereti. In 1433 his father, seeking to strengthen the crown against the great lords, named his four sons Vakhtang, Demetrius, George and Zaal co-rulers. After Alexander's abdication in 1442 Vakhtang IV became King of Kings, and George governed Kakheti, Hereti and neighbouring lands under him with the title of mtavari, prince. On Vakhtang's death in 1446 he took the throne and drove his elder brother Demetrius into exile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Although sovereign in name over the whole realm, George held real power only in Kartli. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a> the atabeg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qvarqvare-ii-jaqeli">Qvarqvare II Jaqeli</a> overthrew his brother Aghbugha II in 1447, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardzia">Vardzia</a> and royal estates, and proclaimed a separate church. George restored Shirvan to tribute after besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qabala">Qabala</a>, while in 1456 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> ravaged Somkhiti and Kartli. He did not intervene when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> fell in 1453 or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> in 1461.</p><p>After the papal nuncio Ludovico da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 1456, George corresponded from 1459 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a>, the Doge Pasquale Malipiero and Duke Philip III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> about a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, but European states held back. His cousin Bagrat, whom he had made Duke of Samokalako in 1455, then rebelled with the backing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a>, Mingrelia, Guria, Abkhazia and Svaneti. While George was occupied in Samtskhe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a>'s generals defeated the Georgian army and laid waste to eastern Georgia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Held by the atabeg until early 1466, George was deposed when Bagrat was crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> as Bagrat VI that February. He won his freedom by promising to pardon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a>, guarantee its autonomy and give up his claims in the west, and was crowned King of Kakheti at the Bodbe Monastery. Allied with the peasantry, the lesser nobility and, from about 1467, with Bagrat against Constantine, he secured recognition of Kakheti as a kingdom. His reforms there curbed the nobles and brought a long peace. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartlis-tskhovreba">The Georgian Chronicles</a> confuse his reign, and the division of the kingdom was formally recognised only in 1490.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George VIII” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Regional lords pressed for independence: Bagrat of Imereti defeated <b>George VIII</b> at Chikhori in 1463, and the atabeg Qvarqvare II of Samtskhe captured him in 1465.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Two battles fought at Chikhori shaped Imereti’s history: Bagrat’s victory over <b>George VIII</b> in 1463 and Solomon I’s victory over the Ottomans in 1757.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
